      "text": "```\nLAYERED STRUCTURE OF ECONOMICS:"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#004",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:economics:00-overview, git-history:economics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "economics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "economics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:economics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Economics and Game Theory - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MICROECONOMICS — atomic building blocks\n  Individual optimization: max U(x) s.t. budget; max π = pQ − C(Q)\n  Market equilibrium: supply = demand → price vector clears all markets\n  Efficiency: First Welfare Theorem — competitive equilibrium is Pareto optimal\n         ↓ extends to\nINFORMATION ECONOMICS — micro with private types\n  Adverse selection, moral hazard, signaling\n  When agents have asymmetric info, competitive equilibrium can collapse\n  (Akerlof lemons; Spence signaling; Mirrlees optimal taxation)\n         ↓ private types + strategic agents =\nGAME THEORY — strategic interaction (multiple optimizing agents)\n  Each agent's optimal action depends on others' strategies\n  Solution concepts: Nash equilibrium, SPE, PBE, BNE\n  Game theory does NOT design the rules; it analyzes given rules\n         ↓ flip the question: design rules to achieve target outcome\nMECHANISM DESIGN — \"inverse game theory\" / algorithmic incentive engineering\n  Social planner chooses rules given agents will play Nash/Bayes-Nash\n  Revelation principle: restricts search to direct truthful mechanisms\n  VCG (efficient outcomes), Myerson (revenue-optimal), DA (stable matching)\n  Connects directly to: distributed systems incentive design, auction platforms\n         ↓ zoom out from individual markets to economy-wide dynamics\nMACROECONOMICS — aggregate phenomena\n  Not just \"big micro\" — emergent dynamics from coordination failures,\n  sticky prices, monetary/fiscal policy, capital accumulation\n  IS-LM, AS-AD, Solow, DSGE models"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#005",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:economics:00-overview, git-history:economics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "economics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "economics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:economics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "economics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "economics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Economics and Game Theory - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CROSS-LINKS:\n  Information economics → mechanism design (private types are the input)\n  Game theory ↔ mechanism design (same math, opposite direction)\n  Macro uses micro foundations (DSGE = game theory + optimization + equilibrium)\n  Information economics ↔ macro (credit markets, bank runs, asymmetric info cycles)\n```"
    },
